 315:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** /**
 316:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * @defgroup CO_critical_sections Critical sections
 317:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * @{
 318:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
 319:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Protection of critical sections in multi-threaded operation.
 320:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
 321:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * CANopenNode is designed to run in different threads, as described in [README.md](index.html). Th
 322:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * differently in different systems. In microcontrollers threads are interrupts with different prio
 323:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * It is necessary to protect sections, where different threads access to the same resource. In sim
 324:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * interrupts or scheduler may be temporary disabled between access to the shared resource. Otherwi
 325:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * semaphores can be used.
 326:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
 327:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * #### Reentrant functions
 328:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Functions CO_CANsend() from C_driver.h, and CO_error() from CO_Emergency.h may be called from di
 329:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Critical sections must be protected. Either by disabling scheduler or interrupts or by mutexes o
 330:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Lock/unlock macro is called with pointer to CAN module, which may be used inside.
 331:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
 332:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * #### Object Dictionary variables
 333:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * In general, there are two threads, which accesses OD variables: mainline (initialization, storag
 334:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * timer (PDO access). CANopenNode uses locking mechanism, where SDO server (or other mainline code
 335:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * of the real-time thread at the moment it reads or writes OD variable. CO_LOCK_OD(CAN_MODULE) and
 336:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * CO_UNLOCK_OD(CAN_MODULE) macros are used to protect:
 337:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - Whole real-time thread,
 338:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - SDO server protects read/write access to OD variable.   Locking of long OD variables, not acce
 339:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *   thread, may   block RT thread.
 340:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - Any mainline code, which accesses PDO-mappable OD variable, must protect   read/write with loc
 341:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *   OD_mappable() for check.
 342:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - Other cases, where non-PDO-mappable OD variable is used inside real-time   thread by some othe
 343:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *   application must be considered with   special care. Also when there are multiple threads acces
 344:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *   (e.g. when using a RTOS), you should always lock the OD.
 345:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
 346:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * #### Synchronization functions for CAN receive
 347:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * After CAN message is received, it is pre-processed in CANrx_callback(), which copies some data i
 348:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * object and at the end sets **new_message** flag. This flag is then pooled in another thread, whi
 349:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * the message. The problem is, that compiler optimization may shuffle memory operations, so it is 
 350:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * that **new_message** flag is surely set at the end. It is necessary to use [Memory
 351:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * barrier](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_barrier).
 352:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
 353:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * If receive function runs inside IRQ, no further synchronization is needed. Otherwise, some kind 
 354:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * has to be included. The following example uses GCC builtin memory barrier `__sync_synchronize()`
 355:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * can be found [here](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/982129/what-does-sync-synchronize-do#982
 356:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  */
 357:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** 
 358:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_LOCK_CAN_SEND(CAN_MODULE)   /**< Lock critical section in CO_CANsend() */
 359:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_UNLOCK_CAN_SEND(CAN_MODULE) /**< Unlock critical section in CO_CANsend() */
 360:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_LOCK_EMCY(CAN_MODULE)       /**< Lock critical section in CO_errorReport() or CO_errorRe
 361:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_UNLOCK_EMCY(CAN_MODULE)     /**< Unlock critical section in CO_errorReport() or CO_error
 362:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_LOCK_OD(CAN_MODULE)         /**< Lock critical section when accessing Object Dictionary 
 363:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_UNLOCK_OD(CAN_MODULE)       /**< Unock critical section when accessing Object Dictionary
 364:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** 
 365:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** /** Check if new message has arrived */
ARM GAS  C:\Users\Lenovo\AppData\Local\Temp\ccOvLNZW.s 			page 16


 366:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_FLAG_READ(rxNew)            ((rxNew) != NULL)
 367:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** /** Set new message flag */
 368:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_FLAG_SET(rxNew)                                                                         
 369:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****     {                                                                                              
 370:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****         __sync_synchronize();                                                                      
 371:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****         rxNew = (void*)1L;                                                                         
 372:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****     }
 373:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** /** Clear new message flag */
 374:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #define CO_FLAG_CLEAR(rxNew)                                                                       
 375:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****     {                                                                                              
 376:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****         __sync_synchronize();                                                                      
 377:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****         rxNew = NULL;                                                                              
 378:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****     }
 379:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** 
 380:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** /** @} */
 381:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** #endif /* CO_DOXYGEN */
